  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA significant shift from the ongoing mild/benign weather is on track \nfor Friday as an upper level trough moving onshore into CA/NV today \nlifts north across MT ahead of an eastward moving trough from \nnorthern Pacific. \n\nThe trough lifting out of CA/NV will spread unusually deep moisture \nfor this time of year (PWATs>200% of normal) north into the area \ntonight through Friday morning with precipitation developing across \nwestern portions of the forecast area (mainly W of I-15) tonight as \nthis moisture is drawn north along a deformation axis across the \nNorthern Rockies. This precipitation will be a mix of decaying \nconvection that develops over the Great Basin this afternoon with \nadditional lift provided by upper jet energy lifting north from the \nGreat Basin as well as in advance of the trough arriving from the \nNW. \n\nThe upper trough approaching from the NW crosses the Northern \nRockies Friday afternoon, shifting the axis of precipitation east \nacross the area while also bringing cooler air aloft and lowering \nsnow levels before precipitation decreases from west to east Friday \nevening. Cooling aloft will support an initial drop in snow levels \nfrom above 9000 ft to around 6000-7000 ft across most of the area by \nFriday afternoon, resulting in a change to wet snow over the \nmountains. A secondary push of lower level cooling surges southeast \nout of AB as the northern upper trough axis emerges east of the \nRockies and with surface low pressure deepening across eastern MT. \nSnow levels quickly lower to 3000-4000 ft across N-central MT as \nearly as late Friday morning across Glacier county and by late \nFriday afternoon across eastern portions of north-central/central MT \nand Friday evening across SW MT valleys. Gusty to locally strong \nnorthwest winds develop with the surge of cooler air across N-\ncentral MT Friday, driven mostly by the surface pressure gradient \nbehind the intensifying low to the east. \n\nMainly dry conditions follow for the weekend into at least early \nnext week with temperatures remaining somewhat cooler than average \nfor late October with afternoon temps in the mid-upper 40s and \novernight lows in the 20s. A more unsettled period may develop by \nmid to late next week as the next trough digging southward along the \nwest coast begins to shift inland, though there there is still \nconsiderable uncertainty with timing and details at this point. \nHoenisch \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nPrecipitation Impacts Tonight Through Friday Morning:\n\nInstances of locally moderate/heavy precipitation rates are likely \ntonight through Friday morning with some embedded convective \nelements and even some isolated thunder possible. \n\nPrecipitation Type and Winter Weather Impacts:\n\nThere is still some uncertainty in the how quickly the lower level \ncooling can change precipitation to snow across lower elevations of \nnorth-central MT Friday afternoon. For most areas outside of the \nmountains, the transition to wet snow will occur with still warm \nground temps and little opportunity for accumulation before \nprecipitation ends Friday evening. Across Judith Basin and Fergus \ncounties however there is some concern for the transition to snow \ncoinciding with gusty northwest winds and potential for upslope \nenhancement, particularly for near Judith Gap, Lewistown and areas \nfrom Raynesford to Geyser. With potential for a quick 1-2 inches of \nsnowfall occurring near/shortly after sunset Friday and potential \nfor gusty winds to add to visibility reduction, winter weather \nadvisories were added for these areas.  \n\nWind Impacts:\n\nStrongest northwest winds on Friday are expected near the Canadian \nborder and areas where winds are enhanced near the Island Mtn \nRanges, particularly the Sweet Grass Hills, Bears Paw, Judith and\nSnowy Ranges, where probabilities for gusts in excess of 55 mph \nare 60-80% and a High Wind Warning has been issued. Gusts in \nexcess of 40 mph are likely across most of north-cenrtal MT Friday\nafternoon, which when combined with temperatures falling to the \n30s will add to the raw conditions for those outdoors. Hoenisch",
